{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The current implementation has clear bugs:\n\n1. It decodes the entire buffer and clears it — so partial lines at the end of a chunk (records not yet terminated by `\\n`) get emitted prematurely (and would fail `json.loads` or emit incomplete records).\n2. UTF-8 multi-byte sequences split across chunk boundaries will raise `UnicodeDecodeError` since `self.buf.decode()` decodes partial bytes.\n3. Blank lines are ignored via `if line:` — that part is fine (blank lines ignored), though `if line` on a stripped? Actually a line with spaces would be non-empty and fail json.loads. The task says \"Blank lines are ignored\" — an empty string line. A line of whitespace probably... Let me be careful. The spec: emit only complete JSONL records, blank lines ignored. A line consisting of whitespace only — JSONL records should be valid JSON. `json.loads(\"   \")` raises. Should we strip whitespace? The task says \"Blank lines are ignored.\" I'll interpret blank lines as empty lines. But to be robust, whitespace-only lines... Hmm. In standard JSONL processing, a line that is whitespace-only is often also skipped. The safest interpretation: skip lines that are empty after stripping? But then a line with just spaces is \"blank\". I think stripping is reasonable and robust: `line.strip()` — but careful, JSON records themselves might have leading/trailing spaces which json.loads handles fine anyway. So skipping lines where `line.strip()` is empty is safe and robust.\n\nActually, let me think about the simplest robust approach:\n\nUse an incremental UTF-8 decoder (`codecs.getincrementaldecoder('utf-8')()` or `codecs.IncrementalDecoder`) to handle codepoints crossing chunk boundaries. Then buffer the decoded text, split on `\\n`, keep the last incomplete segment as remainder, emit complete lines.\n\nAlso need to handle `\\r\\n`? The task says JSONL records — JSONL is newline-delimited, typically `\\n`. Should I handle `\\r\\n`? JSON Lines spec says lines are separated by `\\n`, and `\\r\\n` is also permitted? The JSON Lines spec: \"Lines are separated by \\n. \\r\\n is also supported.\" Hmm, actually the official jsonlines.org spec says: \"JSONL text... Each Line is a Valid JSON Value... Line separator is '\\n'... The last character in the file may be a line separator.\" It doesn't mention \\r\\n explicitly in the main spec, but many implementations tolerate \\r. The task statement says nothing about \\r. Adding `\\r` handling via `.strip()` on each line would be harmless: `json.loads` itself tolerates surrounding whitespace, and stripping handles `\\r`. I'll strip each line — that handles both blank-line detection and stray `\\r`.\n\nHmm, but wait — could stripping break anything? A JSON value is not sensitive to leading/trailing whitespace. So `json.loads(line.strip())` == `json.loads(line)`. Safe.\n\nEdge cases:\n- Chunk boundaries inside a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence → incremental decoder with `final=False` buffers incomplete bytes; on a later chunk completes.\n- Record crossing chunks → we only emit lines terminated by `\\n`.\n- Final chunk without trailing newline: should we emit or hold? The task says \"emit only complete JSONL records\". A record without a trailing newline is technically incomplete (we don't know if more data is coming). The typical expected behavior: hold it in buffer until a newline arrives. Should there be a flush() method? The public API is just `__init__` and `feed`. I shouldn't add API unless needed. The task only mentions `feed`. Holding the incomplete tail is correct — we can't know it's complete. Test only tests a complete line with `\\n`.\n\nHmm, but is there a hidden test that feeds a final chunk without trailing newline and expects it emitted? The task says \"must emit only complete JSONL records\" — a record not terminated by newline is arguably incomplete. The safest production behavior for a stream parser is to buffer until newline. I'll go with buffering. That matches \"emit only complete records\".\n\nEfficiency: avoid O(n²) quadratic buffer accumulation. Using a list of chunks + join is amortized fine. Simple approach with incremental decoder:\n\n```python\nimport codecs\nimport json\n\n\nclass JsonlStream:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._decoder = codecs.getincrementaldecoder(\"utf-8\")()\n        self._buf = \"\"\n\n    def feed(self, chunk):\n        self._buf += self._decoder.decode(chunk)\n        out = []\n        lines = self._buf.split(\"\\n\")\n        self._buf = lines.pop()  # incomplete tail (no trailing newline)\n        for line in lines:\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line:\n                out.append(json.loads(line))\n        return out\n```\n\nWait — `self._buf += decoded` then split: the += is O(len) each feed which is fine amortized per byte? Actually if buffer grows large without newlines, each feed copies the whole buffer — O(total²) in worst case for a giant single line fed in many chunks. To be truly efficient, keep a list of pending text chunks and only join when a newline appears. But simplicity vs. efficiency: \"Prefer simplest asymptotically efficient robust solution.\"\n\nQuadratic behavior in the pathological case (one huge JSON line streamed in many small chunks) is real. A cleaner approach: keep pending chunks in a list, scan the newest chunk for newlines... but newlines can span? No — newline is a single byte (0x0A in UTF-8, and UTF-8 is self-synchronizing: byte 0x0A only appears as the character U+000A, never as part of a multi-byte sequence). So searching for b\"\\n\" in bytes is safe! That's a key property: in UTF-8, the byte 0x0A never occurs inside a multi-byte codepoint. So we can do line splitting on bytes and decode each complete line separately. That avoids decoder-state juggling for lines, but we still need to handle multi-byte codepoints crossing boundaries — decoding each complete line individually handles tha