newline. I'll go with buffering. That matches \"emit only complete records\".\n\nEfficiency: avoid O(n²) quadratic buffer accumulation. Using a list of chunks + join is amortized fine. Simple approach with incremental decoder:\n\n```python\nimport codecs\nimport json\n\n\nclass JsonlStream:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._decoder = codecs.getincrementaldecoder(\"utf-8\")()\n        self._buf = \"\"\n\n    def feed(self, chunk):\n        self._buf += self._decoder.decode(chunk)\n        out = []\n        lines = self._buf.split(\"\\n\")\n        self._buf = lines.pop()  # incomplete tail (no trailing newline)\n        for line in lines:\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line:\n                out.append(json.loads(line))\n        return out\n```\n\nWait — `self._buf += decoded` then split: the += is O(len) each feed which is fine amortized per byte? Actually if buffer grows large without newlines, each feed copies the whole buffer — O(total²) in worst case for a giant single line fed in many chunks. To be truly efficient, keep a list of pending text chunks and only join when a newline appears. But simplicity vs. efficiency: \"Prefer simplest asymptotically efficient robust solution.\"\n\nQuadratic behavior in the pathological case (one huge JSON line streamed in many small chunks) is real. A cleaner approach: keep pending chunks in a list, scan the newest chunk for newlines... but newlines can span? No — newline is a single byte (0x0A in UTF-8, and UTF-8 is self-synchronizing: byte 0x0A only appears as the character U+000A, never as part of a multi-byte sequence). So searching for b\"\\n\" in bytes is safe! That's a key property: in UTF-8, the byte 0x0A never occurs inside a multi-byte codepoint. So we can do line splitting on bytes and decode each complete line separately. That avoids decoder-state juggling for lines, but we still need to handle multi-byte codepoints crossing boundaries — decoding each complete line individually handles that, 